1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,775
Welcome to lectures twenty nine and thirty:

2
00:00:03,775 --> 00:00:07,023
from E D A to a trustworthy pipeline.

3
00:00:07,023 --> 00:00:16,505
Hypotheses, train and test, cross-validation, data leakage, skewed data, and turning analysis into insights.

4
00:00:16,505 --> 00:00:20,280
This first video is about testing honestly.

5
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:24,664
Lecture twenty nine is about testing honestly:

6
00:00:24,664 --> 00:00:32,612
hypothesis refinement, train and test logic, cross-validation, and data leakage and how to prevent it.

7
00:00:32,612 --> 00:00:35,963
Lecture thirty is about reporting honestly:

8
00:00:35,963 --> 00:00:43,443
sampling skewed data, translating E D A into insights, and a course review of the full pipeline.

9
00:00:43,443 --> 00:00:45,625
Our data is Online Shoppers:

10
00:00:45,625 --> 00:00:50,456
twelve thousand three hundred thirty sessions, target Revenue.

11
00:00:51,256 --> 00:00:58,040
Hypothesis refinement turns a hunch into something data can say yes or no to.

12
00:00:58,040 --> 00:00:58,569
Vague:

13
00:00:58,569 --> 00:01:00,243
page value matters.

14
00:01:00,243 --> 00:01:01,036
Specific:

15
00:01:01,036 --> 00:01:05,177
sessions that saw a valued page buy more often.

16
00:01:05,177 --> 00:01:05,970
Testable:

17
00:01:05,970 --> 00:01:11,344
buy rate when page values is above zero versus equal to zero.

18
00:01:11,344 --> 00:01:12,578
Decision rule:

19
00:01:12,578 --> 00:01:17,247
chi-square p below point oh five means keep the idea.

20
00:01:17,247 --> 00:01:18,040
The rule:

21
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:24,472
write the hypothesis and the decision rule before you look at the result.

22
00:01:25,272 --> 00:01:27,113
Two hypotheses tested.

23
00:01:27,113 --> 00:01:27,616
H one:

24
00:01:27,616 --> 00:01:30,294
a valued page means more buying.

25
00:01:30,294 --> 00:01:42,095
Sessions with page values of zero buy at three point nine percent; above zero, fifty six point three percent; p is essentially zero, so keep.

26
00:01:42,095 --> 00:01:42,597
H two:

27
00:01:42,597 --> 00:01:44,439
new visitors buy more.

28
00:01:44,439 --> 00:01:59,337
New visitors buy at twenty four point nine percent, other at nineteen point eight, returning at fourteen point one, with p about four times ten to the minus twenty nine, so keep.

29
00:01:59,337 --> 00:02:02,517
Both pass the rule, and H one is huge:

30
00:02:02,517 --> 00:02:08,376
a session that saw a valued page is fourteen times more likely to buy.

31
00:02:09,176 --> 00:02:14,533
Train and test logic is like practice questions versus the final exam.

32
00:02:14,533 --> 00:02:18,360
The model studies the training rows, the practice.

33
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:23,411
The test rows are the exam, locked away and used once, at the end.

34
00:02:23,411 --> 00:02:31,753
Stratify so both parts keep the same share of buyers, and fix the random state so anyone gets the same split.

35
00:02:31,753 --> 00:02:43,616
Here nine thousand seven hundred sixty four training rows and two thousand four hundred forty one test rows, with fifteen point six percent buyers in both.

36
00:02:44,416 --> 00:02:50,197
Five-fold cross-validation gives five mini-exams inside the training rows.

37
00:02:50,197 --> 00:02:55,431
Each fold takes a turn as the check set while the other four learn.

38
00:02:55,431 --> 00:03:06,368
The five R O C A U C scores are about point eight eight to point nine oh two, a mean of point eight nine with a spread of point oh oh seven.

39
00:03:06,368 --> 00:03:08,555
A small spread means stable.

40
00:03:08,555 --> 00:03:15,664
One split can be lucky; five give a mean and a spread, and the test set is still untouched.

41
00:03:16,464 --> 00:03:22,790
Data leakage means the model sees information it will not have when used for real.

42
00:03:22,790 --> 00:03:23,947
Target leakage:

43
00:03:23,947 --> 00:03:29,965
a feature created after the outcome, like payment confirmed to predict buying.

44
00:03:29,965 --> 00:03:32,202
Train and test contamination:

45
00:03:32,202 --> 00:03:37,756
the same row in both parts, like our one hundred twenty five duplicates.

46
00:03:37,756 --> 00:03:39,454
Preprocessing leakage:

47
00:03:39,454 --> 00:03:45,239
a scaler, imputer or feature selection fitted on all rows before the split.

48
00:03:45,239 --> 00:03:46,860
And sampling leakage:

49
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:52,568
over-sampling before the split, so copies of a row land in train and test.

50
00:03:52,568 --> 00:03:58,200
The result is great scores in the notebook and poor results in real life.

51
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,238
Here is leakage on real data:

52
00:04:01,238 --> 00:04:03,476
the same model in two orders.

53
00:04:03,476 --> 00:04:09,572
Over-sample then split gives a recall of point nine nine two, the leaky number.

54
00:04:09,572 --> 00:04:16,517
Split then over-sample the training rows only gives point six six five, the honest number.

55
00:04:16,517 --> 00:04:28,324
Ninety eight point four percent of the leaky test buyers had an exact copy in the training set, so the model was tested on rows it had already memorised.

56
00:04:28,324 --> 00:04:34,112
The honest point six six five is lower, and it is the number you can trust.

57
00:04:34,912 --> 00:04:36,965
How to prevent leakage.

58
00:04:36,965 --> 00:04:39,106
Remove duplicates first.

59
00:04:39,106 --> 00:04:43,212
Split first, before any fitting or resampling.

60
00:04:43,212 --> 00:04:46,514
Fit every step on training rows only.

61
00:04:46,514 --> 00:04:50,797
Resample, over or under, the training rows only.

62
00:04:50,797 --> 00:04:52,671
Ask of every feature:

63
00:04:52,671 --> 00:04:55,438
is it known at prediction time?

64
00:04:55,438 --> 00:04:57,758
And use the test set once.

65
00:04:57,758 --> 00:05:11,680
A pipeline, like make pipeline with a standard scaler and logistic regression, re-fits the scaler inside each cross-validation fold, so there is no peeking.

